.\"
.\" nfsd-iomode-advisor(8)
.\"
.TH NFSD-IOMODE-ADVISOR 8 "15 October 2026"
.SH NAME
nfsd-iomode-advisor \- recommend NFSD IO modes for an export
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nfsd-iomode-advisor
.RB [ \-i
.IR seconds ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-j ]
.I export
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nfsd-iomode-advisor
measures page cache and memory reclaim metrics on an NFS server.
It then recommends values for the
.B riomode=
and
.B wiomode=
export options of
.IR export .
See
.BR exports (5).
.PP
Run the command on the NFS server while clients use the export with a
typical workload.
Run it while the export uses buffered IO.
With dontcache or direct IO, the page cache metrics are not meaningful.
.PP
The command collects these metrics at the start and the end of the
interval:
.IP \(bu 2
The bytes that NFSD read and wrote for each export, from
.IR /proc/fs/nfsd/export_stats .
.IP \(bu
The bytes that storage supplied for the READs of the export
.RI ( io_read_storage
in
.IR export_stats ,
on a kernel with
.BR CONFIG_TASK_IO_ACCOUNTING ).
Without this value, the command uses the bytes that the block device
of the export read.
The command divides this value by the bytes that NFSD read.
The result is the miss ratio.
.IP \(bu
Page reclaim and refault counters from
.IR /proc/vmstat .
.IP \(bu
Memory pressure from
.IR /proc/pressure/memory .
.IP \(bu
The CPU time of the kswapd and kcompactd kernel threads.
.IP \(bu
Dirty and Writeback memory from
.IR /proc/meminfo .
.IP \(bu
The average READ and WRITE size, from
.IR /proc/net/rpc/nfsd .
.IP \(bu
Optionally, the page cache state of files in the export, from
.BR cachestat (2).
.PP
The command also checks if the filesystem of the export supports
dontcache and direct IO.
If it does not, NFSD uses buffered IO for these modes, and the command
does not recommend them.
.PP
For each recommendation, the command shows the reasons.
If the data is not sufficient, the recommendation is "unknown".
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-interval " seconds
Set the measurement interval.
The default is 60 seconds.
.TP
.BI "\-s, \-\-sample\-files " N
Use
.BR cachestat (2)
on the
.I N
largest files in the export.
The command examines a limited number of files to find them.
The default is 0, which disables this check.
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-json
Show the metrics and the recommendation as JSON.
.SH NOTES
The recommendations come from heuristic thresholds.
Use them as a starting point.
Change the IO mode of one export at a time, and compare the metrics
before and after the change.
.PP
The miss ratio includes the readahead and the filesystem metadata
that the READs needed.
A miss ratio larger than 1 shows that readahead reads data that clients
do not request.
.PP
The block device miss ratio is approximate.
Local applications also cause device reads.
If more than one export is on the same block device, this miss ratio
is for all of these exports.
.PP
The kernel keeps the counters of an export when mountd refreshes it.
If an export cache entry appears during the interval with counters
that started earlier, its traffic in the interval is not known, and
the command does not include it.
.PP
The average IO sizes are for all exports.
The memory metrics are for the full system.
.PP
The command must run as root to read the global IO modes in debugfs
and to open the files for
.BR cachestat (2).
.SH FILES
.I /proc/fs/nfsd/export_stats
.br
.I /proc/net/rpc/nfsd
.br
.I /proc/net/rpc/nfsd.export/content
.br
.I /sys/kernel/debug/nfsd/io_cache_read
.br
.I /sys/kernel/debug/nfsd/io_cache_write
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR exports (5),
.BR exportfs (8),
.BR nfsiostat (8),
.BR mountstats (8),
.BR cachestat (2)
